1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,050
Listen to a talk in an art history class.

2
00:00:05,050 --> 00:00:08,040
Man: When we think of the Renaissance

3
00:00:08,580 --> 00:00:12,010
we often picture grand paintings and sculptures.

4
00:00:12,010 --> 00:00:18,100
But a lesser known art form also flourished during this period-tapestry weaving.

5
00:00:18,100 --> 00:00:21,070
Tapestries were not only decorative pieces,

6
00:00:21,070 --> 00:00:23,900
but also served practical purposes

7
00:00:24,380 --> 00:00:28,000
such as insulating the cold stone walls of castles.

8
00:00:28,000 --> 00:00:34,397
Artists of the era used tapestries to depict intricate scenes from mythology,

9
00:00:34,397 --> 00:00:40,260
religion, and daily life, often incorporating vibrant colors and rich textures.

10
00:00:40,260 --> 00:00:44,100
An advantage of tapestries was their portability.

11
00:00:44,100 --> 00:00:47,023
Unlike frescoes, which were fixed to walls,

12
00:00:47,023 --> 00:00:52,034
tapestries could be rolled up and transported from one location to another,

13
00:00:52,034 --> 00:00:56,210
allowing their owners to display their wealth wherever they went.

14
00:00:56,210 --> 00:01:00,280
This portability also had a downside, however

15
00:01:00,900 --> 00:01:04,080
Tapestries were susceptible to damage during transit.

16
00:01:04,080 --> 00:01:12,020
Even today, museums face challenges preserving these unique artworks due to their fragile nature.

17
00:01:12,020 --> 00:01:22,010
The creation of a tapestry was a labor-intensive process requiring collaboration between designers, weavers, and dyers.

18
00:01:22,010 --> 00:01:29,060
Unlike paintings, which could be completed by a single artist, tapestries were team efforts.

19
00:01:29,060 --> 00:01:36,070
And it was common for a designer's sketches to be translated by skilled weavers into woven masterpieces.

20
00:01:36,070 --> 00:01:47,120
The intricate craftsmanship required to produce these artworks offers a glimpse into the collaborative spirit of the Renaissance era.
